    "mission_1.compare.arc": "The robot turned left as predicted, but the final heading was 1.519 rad instead of 1.600 rad, a difference of 0.081 rad. This could be because the robot did not turn at exactly 0.40 rad/s. The odometry speed and timing data could help check this.\n",
    "mission_1.compare.straight": "My prediction was that the robot would travel straight forward for 0.450 m with no change in heading. The odometry result was a final x-position of 0.418 m, which is 0.032 m less than predicted. The final y-position and heading matched the prediction at 0.000 m and 0.000 radians.\n\nOne possible cause of the difference is that the robot traveled slightly less than the commanded distance due to timing, acceleration, or wheel slip. Evidence that would help check this cause would be the recorded odometry velocity and timing data to see whether the robot actually maintained 0.15 m/s for the full 3 seconds.",
    "mission_1.compare.turn_then_drive": "My prediction was 0.300 m forward after turning left 90\u00b0. The robot actually traveled 0.277 m, which was 0.023 m less than predicted. This could be caused by the robot moving slightly slower than expected. The odometry speed and timing data could help confirm this.",
    "mission_1.error.arc": "",
    "mission_1.error.straight": "",
    "mission_1.error.turn_then_drive": "",
    "mission_1.hallway_analysis": "Curved or turning motion could confuse someone nearby because the robot's direction is less predictable. I would reduce the turning speed, which would make the robot easier to predict but would take longer to change direction. One measurable criterion is keeping a safe passing distance of at least 1 meter. This could be evaluated by measuring the closest distance between the robot and a person during the motion.\n",
    "mission_1.predictions": {
      "arc": {
        "description": "The robot will turn left while moving forward, following a curved path. It will finish facing mostly backward-left.",
